                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frank+Adams"} Frank Adams
            in his Infinite Loop Spaces book (since there is rather a lot of topological flab in the definition).
          %h2#basic_idea Basic idea
          %p
            An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} arrow
            looks like an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+object"} interval
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} theory of categories
            and even
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} n-categories
            should have a lot to do with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+object"} interval
            — especially when it comes to applications to
            = succeed "!" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
          %p
            In a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            we can glue
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} arrows
            together, ‘composing’ them. But an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+object"} interval
            can be chopped apart or ‘decomposed’ into a bunch of intervals. So, there should be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocategory"} cocategory
            or something like that lurking around here.
          %p
            In fact the closed unit interval gives an
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-infinity+category"} A-infinity
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocategory"} cocategory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocategory"} cocategory
            where the laws hold up to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+coherent+category+theory"} ad infinitum
          %p
            The space of maps out of an
            = succeed "-cocategory" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-infinity+category"} A-infinity category
            = succeed "-category." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Todd+Trimble"} Todd Trimble
            constructs the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+infinity-groupoid"} fundamental n-groupoid
            of a space!
          %p
            But the really cool part is how this construction goes hand in hand with his definition of
            = succeed "-categories," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %h2#history History
          %p
            In 1999,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Todd+Trimble"} Todd Trimble
            introduced a definition of weak
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-groupoid," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Leinster"} Tom Leinster
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eugenia+Cheng"} Eugenia Cheng
            = succeed "\u2018s)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Batanin"} Batanin
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
          %p Trimble never published this material himself (in effect leaving it to others present at the Cambridge lecture to develop their own accounts, which has been a good thing), but it may be of at least historical interest to have Trimble’s original account available. We will then pick out the highlights and streamline the approach, following subsequent work by Leinster and Cheng.
          %h2#the_original_idea The original idea
          %p
            The original idea was to use an
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-infinity-cocategory"} cocategory
            structure on the topological
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+object"} interval
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} topological
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operad"} operad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            which is used to control all ways of weakly composing arrows. Then one proceeds to define
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-category"} weak n-categories
            by induction, defining at each stage a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-groupoid"} n-groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mi n
                  %mo :
                  %mi Top
                  %mo →
                  %mi n
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_n: Top \to n Cat
          %p
            and then defining an
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi nCat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} nCat
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_n(T)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+an+operad"} algebra
            in the category of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n Cat
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph"} graph
          %p
            It will be helpful to review the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental (1-)groupoid
            of a space first, doing it in such a way that we will see how to inductively define the fundamental
            = succeed "-groupoid." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
            denote its underlying set, viewed as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+category"} discrete category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is a category, then a
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              \-graph
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
            is just a functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0 \times X_0 \to V
          %p
            (which here amounts to a function
            = succeed ").There" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ob
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0 \times X_0 \to Ob(V)
            = succeed "-graphs" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
          %ul
            %li
              We can also consider a more general category
              = succeed "," do
                %strong
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                  \-Graph
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C_0, C(-, -)) \to (D_0, D(-, -))
              consists of a function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 0
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_0: C_0 \to D_0
              together with a collection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 0
                    %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 0
                    %mi c
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(c, c') \to D(f_0 c, f_0 c')
              of morphisms in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %p
            A basic example of
            = succeed "-graph" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          #eq:Trimble1.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∼
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X^\sim : X_0 \times X_0 \to Top
          %p
            by letting
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∼
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^\sim(x, y)
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} space
            of paths from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            are homotopic if they lie in the same connected component of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mo ∼
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^\sim(x, y)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mo ∼
                  %mi Top
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mn 0
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0 \times X_0 \stackrel{X^\sim}{\to} Top \stackrel{\Pi_0}{\to} Set
          %p
            we get the graph of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+%28as+a+transformation%29"} homotopy classes
            of paths. What we want is to put a category structure on this
            = succeed "-graph," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(X)
          %p
            The
            = succeed "-graph" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:Trimble1"} (1)
            can also be thought of as a topological
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
            from the discrete space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
            to itself, and this is obviously closely related to the span
          #eq:Trimble2.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi i
                            %mn 0
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi I
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi i
                            %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X^1 \stackrel{X^{i_0}}{\leftarrow} X^I \stackrel{X^{i_1}}{\to} X^1
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi i
                    %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_0, i_1: 1 \to I
            are the endpoint inclusions. Indeed, we get
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:Trimble1"} (1)
            from
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:Trimble2"} (2)
            by first precomposing with a span from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ←
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0 \leftarrow X_0 \to X,
          %p (both arrows being identity functions), followed by postcomposing with the opposite span. This pre- and post-composition gives a functor
          #eq:Trimble3.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (3)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi TopSpan
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi TopSpan
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi TopGraph
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  TopSpan(X, X) \to TopSpan(X_0, X_0) = TopGraph_{X_0}
          %p
            to which we return later, but for the time being we work with the span
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:Trimble2"} (2)
          %p
            As a functor in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:Trimble2"} (2)
            is obtained by homming out of the
            %em interval cospan
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mn 0
                  %mi I
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mn 1
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \stackrel{i_0}{\to} I \stackrel{i_1}{\leftarrow} 1
          %p
            and the fundamental groupoid structure on the span
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:Trimble1"} (1)
            ultimately comes about by homming out of an H-cogroupoid structure on the interval cospan, just as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
            of a based space is obtained by homming out of the pointed circle, seen as an H-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cogroup"}> cogroup
            \.
          %p
            Let us describe the H-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocategory"} cocategory
            structure on the interval
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cospan"} cospan
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+object"} pointed
            space, and the composite of two such cospans
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is obtained by identifying the second point of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with the first point of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \vee Y
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi I
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ∨
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ≅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \vee I = [0, 1] \vee [0, 1] \cong [0, 2]
          %p
            and the co-composition for the H-cocategory structure on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %mi I
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \to I \vee I
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0, 1] \to [0, 2]
          %p
            This co-operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi I
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \to I \vee I
            is not strictly coassociative, but is coassociative up to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+%28as+a+transformation%29"} homotopy
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+space"} convexity
            of the
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi n
                  %mo ≅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^{\vee n} \cong [0, n]
            that the space of cospan maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(I, I^{\vee n})
            is
            = succeed ":" do
              %em contractible
            = succeed "-ladder\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{\infty}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            carries an
            = succeed "-cocategory" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{\infty}
          %p
            But we don’t actually need the
            = succeed "-jargon:" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{\infty}
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                There is a ‘tautological’ topological operad
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                where the component
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_n
                is the space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi I
                      %mo ,
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mrow
                          %mo ∨
                          %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(I, I^{\vee n})
                of cospan maps;
            %li
              %p
                Each component
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_n
                is contractible.
          %p
            The operad structure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is derived from pure abstract nonsense: if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is an object in a monoidal category, then the objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(A, A^{\otimes n})
            are the components of an operad, just as are the objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(A^{\otimes n}, A)
            the components of the familiar tautological endomorphism operad. This applies in particular to the cospan
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            ‘tautological’ as well.
          %p
            Now we just hom out of the cospan co-operations encapsulated in the operad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            to get span operations on the topological span above. That is, we use the facts that
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(-, X)
                takes the
                = succeed "-fold" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mrow
                          %mo ∨
                          %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^{\vee n}
                to the
                = succeed "-fold" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ←
                        %msup
                          %mi X
                          %mi I
                        %mo →
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \leftarrow X^I \to X
            %li
              %p
                Each cospan co-operation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi θ
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mi hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi I
                      %mo ,
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mrow
                          %mo ∨
                          %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta \in T_n = \hom(I, I^{\vee n})
                induces a span operation
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi θ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mrow
                          %mo ∨
                          %mi n
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi I
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(\theta, X): \hom(I^{\vee n}, X) \to \hom(I, X)
              %p
                from the
                = succeed "-fold" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Again, we could express this by saying we derive an
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-infinity-category"} category
            structure on the topological span
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:Trimble2"} (2)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(I, I^{\vee n}) \times \hom(I^{\vee n}, X) \to \hom(I, X)
          %p
            gives a
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          #eq:Trimble4.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (4)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi n
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ←
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ←
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  T_n \times (X \leftarrow X^I \to X)^{\otimes n} \to (X \leftarrow X^I \to X)
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            indicates span composite.
          %ul
            %li
              %strong> Remark
              \:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is an operad in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Top
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi TopSpan
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TopSpan(X, X)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Top
                      %mo →
                      %mi TopSpan
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top \to TopSpan(X, X)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
              to the span
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mover
                        %mo ←
                        %mi π
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Y
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi π
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \stackrel{\pi}{\leftarrow} X \times Y \stackrel{\pi}{\to} X
              %p
                This change of base is (strong) monoidal. As a matter of fact, this change of base factors through the center of the monoidal category
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi TopSpan
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TopSpan
          %p
            We get a similar
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "-graph" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∼
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^\sim: X_0 \times X_0 \to Top
            we started with. Indeed, the functor
          #eq:Trimble3.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (5)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi TopSpan
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi TopSpan
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %msub
                    %mi TopGraph
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  TopSpan(X, X) \to TopSpan(X_0, X_0) \cong TopGraph_{X_0}
          %p
            is a lax monoidal functor, and hence takes
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi TopSpan
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TopSpan(X, X)
            (as in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:Trimble4"} (4)
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi TopGraph
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TopGraph_{X_0}
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          #eq:Trimble5.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (6)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi n
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∼
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  T_n \times (X^\sim)^{\otimes n} \to X^\sim
          %p
            in the monoidal category of
            = succeed "-graphs." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:Trimble5"} (6)
                is the crucial topological input in Trimble’s machine
          %p
            Let us make this more explicit. In general, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is a monoidal category with coproducts, such that the tensor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \otimes v
            preserves coproducts in each of its separate arguments
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            = succeed "-graphs" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "-graphs" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G, H
            we define
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi y
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (G \otimes H)(x, z) = \sum_y G(x, y) \otimes H(y, z)
          %p
            Hence, in the case
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo =
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = Top
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:Trimble5"} (6)
            takes the more explicit form
          #eq:Trimble6.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (7)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi n
                  %mo ×
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∼
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∼
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi …
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∼
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∼
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  T_n \times \sum_{y_1, \ldots, y_{n-1}} X^\sim(x, y_1) \times X^\sim(y_1, y_2) \times \ldots \times X^\sim(y_{n-1}, z) \to X^{\sim}(x, z)
          %p
            Now apply the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo :
                  %mi Top
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0: Top \to Set
            (which preserves products and incidentally sums as well) to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:Trimble6"} (7)
            = succeed "-graph" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 0
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0 X^\sim
            an algebra over the operad
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 0
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0 T
            = succeed "-graph" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 0
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0 T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 0
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0 T_n
            consists of just a point, i.e., is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+operad"} associative operad
            whose algebras are monoids, and a category is a monoid in a category of graphs.
          %p
            This category is precisely the fundamental groupoid
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(X)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(X)
            is functorial in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to Y
            is a continuous map, we can pull back the
            = succeed "-graph" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^\sim
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y_0
            to a
            = succeed "-graph" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ×
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 0
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 0
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Y
                        %mo ∼
                  %mi Top
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0 \times X_0 \stackrel{f_0 \times f_0}{\to} Y_0 \times Y_0 \stackrel{Y^\sim}{\to} Top,
          %p
            which we call
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi f
                      %mn 0
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mi Y
                      %mo ∼
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_0^{-1} Y^\sim
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to Y
            induces a comparison map of
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi TopGraph
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TopGraph_{X_0}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∼
                  %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi f
                    %mn 0
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ∼
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*: X^\sim \to f_0^{-1} Y^\sim: X_0 \times X_0 \to Top
          %p
            and again by composing with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo :
                    %mi Top
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0: Top \to Set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 0
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0 f^*: \Pi_1(X) \to \Pi_1(Y)
          %p
            Thus, after a series of small tautological steps, we have bootstrapped our way up from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1
            to a definition of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_2
            = succeed "-categorical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A (Trimble) 0-category is a set. The fundamental 0-groupoid
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Top
                      %mo →
                      %mn 0
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top \to 0 Cat
                is the connected components functor
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Π
                          %mn 0
                        %mo :
                        %mi Top
                        %mo →
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_0: Top \to Set
            %li
              %p
                For
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 0
                %strong
                  Trimble
                  = succeed "-category" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
                consists of a set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
                and a
                = succeed "-graph" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mi Cat
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n Cat
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ×
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 0
                      %mo →
                      %mi n
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X: X_0 \times X_0 \to n Cat
              %p
                equipped with a structure of algebra over the operad
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mi n
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_n T
                (a priori an operad in the cartesian monoidal category
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mi Cat
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n Cat
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mi Cat
                      %msub
                        %mi Graph
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n Cat Graph_{X_0}
                by an obvious change of base; cf. the remark above).
            %li
              %p
                A
                %strong morphism
                of Trimble
                = succeed "-categories" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi Y
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_0, X) \to (Y_0, Y)
                consists of of a function
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mn 0
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 0
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi Y
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_0: X_0 \to Y_0
                together with a
                %em strict
                = succeed "-algebra" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Π
                          %mi n
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_n T
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo :
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %msubsup
                          %mi f
                          %mn 0
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to f_0^{-1} Y
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)Cat
                admits finite products and arbitrary sums over which products distribute.
            %li
              %p
                The
                %strong
                  fundamental
                  = succeed "-groupoid" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                      %mo :
                      %mi Top
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_{n+1}: Top \to (n+1)Cat
              %p
                sends a space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                to the pair
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi Π
                          %mi n
                        %msup
                          %mi X
                          %mo ∼
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_0, \Pi_n X^\sim)
                = succeed "-algebra" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Π
                          %mi n
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_n T
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_n
                to the tautological
                = succeed "-algebra" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi X
                          %mo ∼
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^\sim
            %li
              %p
                %strong> Theorem
                \:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_{n+1}
                preserves finite products and arbitrary coproducts. (This theorem is needed to push the induction through.)
          %p And that’s it. Notice this approach is in essence globular, that is we have an underlying functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi n
                  %mo :
                  %mi n
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi GlobularSet
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_n: n Cat \to (n)GlobularSet
          %p
            to
            = succeed "-globular" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-globular" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_0, X)
            is given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Cat
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi GlobularSet
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0 \times X_0 \to (n)Cat \stackrel{U_n}{\to} (n)GlobularSet
          %p
            using the fact that an
            = succeed "-globular" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            = succeed "-globular" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            As Cheng put it, Trimble’s inductive definition is disarmingly straightforward, and yet it codes up all the essential structure one expects to see in weak
            = succeed "-categories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1 T_3
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi I
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi I
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(I, I \vee I \vee I)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi δ
                  %mo ∨
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi δ
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi δ
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\delta \vee 1)\delta, (1 \vee \delta)\delta: [0, 1] \to [0, 3]
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta: [0, 1] \to [0, 2]
            is a co-composition. The pentagonator is similarly coded up as a 2-cell in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_2 T_4
          %p To be continued…
          %h2#comparison_to_classical_notions_of_category_in_low_degree
            Comparison to classical notions of
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            A Trimble
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-equivalence," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n-1
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            In comparison with classical concrete definitions of higher category, a bicategory is precisely a Trimble 2-category, but a Trimble 3-category is a tricategory satisfying a strict interchange law for composition.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simpson%27s+conjecture"} Simpson's conjecture
            implies that every tricategory should be equivalent to one with a strict interchange law for composition: this is proven for tricategories with a single object. A different, proven, coherence theorem for tricategories states that every tricategory is equivalent to a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gray-category"} Gray category
            %em except
            for the interchange law.
          %h2#iterative_operadic_enrichment Iterative operadic enrichment
          %p
            The account above was given in a traditional language of operads, but subsequent analysis by Leinster and Cheng led to the following notion, involving a hybrid between algebras over an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operad"} operad
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched categories
          %p
            Fix a symmetric monoidal category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            be an operad valued in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V, P)
              \-category
            consists of
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A set of objects
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
            %li
              %p
                A
                = succeed "-graph" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo :
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 0
                        %mo ×
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 0
                        %mo →
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X(-, -): X_0 \times X_0 \to V
            %li
              %p
                Maps
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi P
                        %mi n
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi …
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_n \otimes X(x_0, x_1) \otimes \ldots X(x_{n-1}, x_n) \to X(x_0, x_n)
                parametrized over all choices
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0, x_1, \ldots, x_n
          %p subject to axioms similar to the usual axioms for algebras over an operad.
          %ul
            %li
              Example: For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Top
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = (Top, \times)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              the operad
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi hom
                      %mi cospan
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi I
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∨
                        %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T = \{\hom_{cospan}(I, I^{\vee n})\}
              discussed above, we constructed a
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
